cors — like a bouncer at a club door

Imagine your API is a toy store, and websites are kids who want to come in and grab toys (data).

js
const cors = require('cors');
app.use(cors({
    origin: 'https://african-frontend.vercel.app',
    methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PATCH', 'DELETE'],
    allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization']
}));
cors is like hiring a bouncer for your toy store.
origin: 'https://african-frontend.vercel.app' — the bouncer says: "Only kids from THIS address are allowed in. Everyone else, go away!" So only your specific frontend website is allowed to talk to your API. If some random website tried to call your API, the browser blocks it.
methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PATCH', 'DELETE'] — the bouncer also says: "You can only look at toys (GET), bring new toys (POST), fix toys (PATCH), or throw toys away (DELETE). Nothing else."
allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'] — this is like saying: "You can only bring these two specific backpacks in with you — nothing extra." (Content-Type tells your API what kind of data is coming in, like "this is JSON". Authorization is where the login token/cookie info goes.)
Why do we even need this?

By default, browsers say: "A website can only talk to its own address, not some other API on a different address — that's suspicious, block it!" This is called the Same-Origin Policy. CORS is you telling the browser: "No no, it's okay, THIS specific website is allowed to talk to my API."

express.urlencoded — a translator for old-fashioned forms
js
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));
Imagine someone sends you a note, but it's written in a weird squished format like:
  name=Sarah&age=20&city=Lagos
express.urlencoded() is a translator that takes that squished note and turns it into a normal, readable object your code can use:
js
  { name: "Sarah", age: "20", city: "Lagos" }
This format (key=value&key=value) is what plain HTML <form> submissions send by default (not JSON) — so this middleware exists specifically to understand that.
{ extended: true } — means: "Also let me understand nested/complicated notes, not just simple flat ones." (Uses a smarter library called qs under the hood, which can handle things like arrays and nested objects, not just flat key-value pairs.)
Quick comparison
Middleware	Understands data shaped like...
express.json()	{ "name": "Sarah" } (JSON — what Postman/React apps usually send)
express.urlencoded()	name=Sarah&age=20 (plain HTML form submissions)
Putting it together, like a story

Your server is a toy store. 🧸

cors = the bouncer at the door, checking who's allowed in and what they're allowed to do
express.json() = a translator for notes written in JSON language
express.urlencoded() = a translator for notes written in old form language

Both translators just make sure that whatever format the message arrives in, your code (req.body) can understand it properly.

