Sure! Here's why that happens, kept simple:

Cookies are shy — they don't like leaving their own house

Think of a cookie (your access token) like a secret note your server hands to a kid (the browser) and says: "Keep this note. Bring it back to ME every time you visit, so I know it's you."

By default, that note has an invisible rule stamped on it:

"Only show this note to the house that gave it to you."

So if your frontend (african-frontend.vercel.app) and your backend/API (maybe myapi.onrender.com) are two different houses (different domains), the browser gets suspicious:

"Wait... this note was written by House B (your API), but I'm currently standing in House A (your frontend) trying to talk to House B. Are you sure I should hand this note over? That seems risky!"

By default, the browser says "nope, not sending it" — unless both sides explicitly say it's okay.

Two-sided permission needed

Both your frontend and backend need to agree to this:

1. Backend (your Express server) says: "Yes, I'm okay receiving cookies from cross-origin requests"

js
app.use(cors({
    origin: 'https://african-frontend.vercel.app',
    credentials: true // <-- "yes, allow cookies"
}));

2. Frontend (React/fetch call) also has to say: "Yes, please include my cookies when you talk to that other house"

js
fetch('https://myapi.onrender.com/app/login', {
    method: 'POST',
    credentials: 'include', // <-- "yes, send my cookies"
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ email, Username, password })
});

If either side forgets their part, the cookie just quietly never shows up — no error message, no crash. Your login POST request will succeed and return 200, but then every protected route afterward will say "invalid token" because the browser never actually delivered the cookie. This confuses a LOT of people because everything looks like it worked.

Bonus: secure and sameSite also matter here

Remember your issueToken.js cookie settings:

js
res.cookie('access', token, {
    httpOnly: true,
    secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production',
    sameSite: 'strict', // <-- this one matters a lot for cross-domain
    maxAge: 7 * 60 * 60 * 1000
});

sameSite: 'strict' means: "Only send this cookie if the request is coming from the EXACT same site." Since your frontend and backend are different domains, 'strict' will block the cookie even with credentials: true set everywhere else.

For a cross-domain setup like yours (Vercel frontend + separate API host), you'll want:

js
sameSite: 'none', // allow cross-site
secure: true       // required when sameSite is 'none' — cookie only sent over HTTPS

⚠️ Important: sameSite: 'none' requires secure: true (HTTPS only) — browsers will reject the cookie otherwise. Since you'll be deploying to Vercel (frontend) and something like Render (backend), both will have HTTPS by default, so this should work fine in production. Just note that secure: true won't work on localhost during local testing unless you're using HTTPS locally too — something to keep in mind if cookies mysteriously stop working when you switch between local dev and deployed testing.


