Exactly — that's precisely it.

The browser stores it automatically, on your frontend code's behalf — without your code ever touching it
Here's the flow:

Backend responds with Set-Cookie: access=eyJhbGc... in the response headers
The browser itself (not your frontend JavaScript code) sees that header and thinks: "Ah, a cookie! Let me store this in my cookie jar for this domain."
Your frontend code (React, whatever) never runs a line like saveCookie(token) — the browser just does it silently, automatically, as part of how HTTP works
Think of it like a mail carrier vs. the homeowner
The browser = the mail carrier
Your frontend code = the homeowner
The cookie = a locked box delivered by mail
The mail carrier (browser) picks up the box from the server, walks it to your house, and puts it in your mailbox (cookie storage) — completely on their own. The homeowner (your frontend JS) never touches the box, never opens it, doesn't even need to know exactly what's inside.

Then later, when your frontend makes another request to the same backend:

The mail carrier (browser) automatically grabs that same locked box from the mailbox and attaches it to the outgoing request
Again, your frontend code doesn't do this manually — it just happens, as long as credentials: 'include' tells the browser "yes, include the mailbox contents with this delivery"
Why this is actually the whole point (security-wise)
This is intentionally designed so that:

Your frontend code can't be tricked (via a malicious script/XSS attack) into reading and stealing the token, since it never has direct access to it
But the browser still automatically proves "this is the same logged-in user" on every request, without your code needing to manually manage tokens at all
So yes — stored, but never read by your frontend code. The browser handles the entire lifecycle (store it, attach it, send it) behind the scenes, and your JavaScript just benefits from the fact that "the user is logged in" without ever seeing how.


